WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: ar

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.680
In the previous episode, we got off on knowing private and public keys, addresses

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.360
and how we can create them. We also knew that we could share the address

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:16.080
or the public key with anyone as he / she could use that address to send us bitcoin or cryptocurrency.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.560
As for the private key, it is secret and can never be known to anyone but you,

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.120
otherwise they may withdraw your crypto-currency balances,

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:30.400
just as if you gave your credit card and PIN to a passing person you met on the street.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:36.040
We also explained that if the private key is lost or forgotten, all the currencies stored

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:43.160
on it are lost forever as there is no specific party that you can turn to to request your wallet's private key.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:49.560
Contrary to the PIN number, for example, if you forgot/lost it, you can go to the bank and extract another new number.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:55.040
So, in mid-2018, according to the Wall Street Journal, Chainalysis,

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:58.034
an American company specializing in blockchain analysis,

00:00:58.034 --> 00:01:02.520
conducted a study in which it revealed the amount of lost bitcoin,

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:08.560
as it reached 20% of all bitcoins in existence at the time due to individuals losing

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:13.840
the private key that we aforementioned. Most of the 20% of those currencies are considered lost forever,

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:18.360
a small percentage of that amount are being tried to be restored by their own owners.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.240
So why is this big percentage all missing? How much is it now?

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:25.240
What is the terminus of this wealth? Is there anybody looking for it?

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.600
These interesting questions are going to be answered in today's episode,

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:34.640
I am Ashraf Ibrahim, and this is our fifth episode of our new series on cryptocurrencies, "Crypto Economic Informant".

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:53.040
In the early 2010s, when the value of one bitcoin was less than $1,

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:56.280
people were not vastly interested in this private key

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:01.720
because the value of the currency was not much to count on. The whole issue was just a new trendy hobby.

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:08.320
Later after that, lots of people tried by leaps and bounds to figure out what their private key was.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:16.240
Some of them were lost forever, which constitutes about one fifth of the entire number of bitcoins in existence, as said.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:20.280
Well, at the time of this talk, how many Bitcoins are there in the world right now?

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:26.920
In other words, how many bitcoins are there starting with the first 50 unspendable bitcoins in the formation block,

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:33.800
which we previously mentioned in episode No. 3 wherein we were talking about the formation block. You can watch it here.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:40.240
So, how many bitcoins are there in the world now? There are about 19 million bitcoins.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:45.960
One-fifth of this number is 3.8 million bitcoins, their owners lost their private key

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:50.840
and thus lost this huge wealth. Do you know how much that One-fifth equal at the time

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:55.800
of writing this episode [during the first half of the current year 2022]?

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:00.240
It is the highest price that bitcoin has ever reached, which is 47 thousand dollars.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:05.760
This means that 3.8 million missing bitcoins, which are one-fifth of the total number of bitcoins,

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:13.520
are worth more than 178 billion dollars. That's the value of bitcoins whose private keys

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:18.520
are somewhere on this planet, but lost. Imagine someone who wrote

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:22.160
his private key on a piece of paper that was scattered or lost in a certain place

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:26.920
amid tons of books for example. Or it may be located on a lost USB flash drive.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:33.240
It may be located inside a damaged hard disk thrown somewhere and no one knows the treasure it contains.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.120
An important question popped up here:

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:38.880
Is there any hope that these people will be able to get their private keys back?

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:43.920
The answer is yes, there is hope, if they know a piece / part of the code used in these keys;

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.840
there is a possibility that they will be able to figure out the unknown part.

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:49.960
That is why a category called wallet hunters appeared:

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:54.520
Their job is to try to help investors or people recover the lost Bitcoin

00:03:54.520 --> 00:04:03.080
by trying to retrieve their private key. These wallet hunters charge fees ranging from 5 to 20%

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:07.880
of the bitcoin that they can redeem. However, these are just attempts.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:10.360
Well what are those wallet hunters doing?

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:13.160
They use different tools that help them in their investigation.

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:17.000
Starting with traditional investigation methods to access the devices

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:22.920
on which the private key might be stored, passing through the testing of a set of different keys

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:27.240
[if the owner of the bitcoins  remembers even a part of the private key for example],

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:31.520
up to the best guess of this private key which is tested by what is so-called

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.400
Brute-force attack or a blind force attack or brute force

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:40.440
which is an attack that uses supercomputers to test a large number of private keys

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:43.800
until they reach the correct one of the investor.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:48.280
Of course, if the owner of this bitcoin cannot remember anything of that private key code,

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:51.720
the possibility of accessing it will be very weak. but if they remember albeit a part

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:55.480
of the code, the probability of recovering the key increases significantly.

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:58.800
Sometimes it even comes to using hypnosis,

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:03.960
there is a type of wallet hunter called Crypto Hypnotist and their job is

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:08.160
to hypnotize the person who lost his/her private key in an attempt to

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:13.520
access the code or even part of it. Despite that, there is an amount of coins that have been retrieved

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:17.400
but it is negligible compared to the amount lost so far,

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.840
so you must resolutely keep your private key and memorize it through and through.

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:26.120
This was a quick review about the lost Bitcoin fortune and the odd realm of wallet hunters.

